export const SERVER_INSTRUCTIONS = "Market data and research from the public sources themselves. Before answering with a company's reported figures, check them with verify_claims: each comes back confirmed, contradicted (with the filed figure and what the wrong one matches) or not reported. Across the market: screen_companies ranks every US-listed company on fundamentals, company_report gives one company's financials, segment_revenue its revenue by segment, product and region, valuation, risk, insiders and filings in one call, event_study measures price reactions to earnings or insider trades, mentions_trend counts a phrase in filings over time. Analyst work: financial_statements gives the income statement, balance sheet and cash flows exactly as filed (several years joined, restatements and splits shown, never mixed); filing_tables finds the table that holds a figure in a filing, or follows it through several years of filings (members, stores, ARPU, guidance tables); earnings_release reads a results press release: results, guidance as ranges, and last quarter's guidance against this quarter's results. Companies: company_profile, list_filings, then read_filing (section \"risk factors\", \"md&a\" and so on for a 10-K or 10-Q; document \"EX-99.1\" for an 8-K press release); search_filings finds words across all filings; executive_pay reads a proxy's pay-versus-performance table; company_events gives a company's 8-K news (or, by event, the whole market's: restatements, bankruptcies, cyber incidents); latest_filings is what has just been filed, across every company, by form and event; filing_diff shows what changed in a 10-K's risk factors (or any item) since the last one; filing_drift follows a section over several years (how much its wording moved, hedging, readability, and a theme such as \"supply chain\"), flagging the years and themes that moved most; news gives the world's press coverage of a company or topic, with its tone; explain_move puts a day's move beside the filings and headlines around it; portfolio_xray opens a portfolio's funds into the companies they hold; comps sets a company beside its industry peers; debt_profile gives its debt, when it falls due and what it costs; ownership_breadth counts the institutions holding it, quarter by quarter; forensic_scores runs the Beneish, Piotroski and Altman screens on its filings; reverse_dcf solves for the growth its share price implies; shareholder_returns what it has paid its owners each year (dividends, buybacks, the share count, payout ratios, its dividend record and yields); comment_letters gives what the SEC questioned in its filings and how it answered; federal_contracts what the US government paid it (USAspending); drug_pipeline a drug company's active trials by phase, its next readouts and its FDA approvals; bank_financials a US bank's quarterly call-report condition (uninsured deposits, bad loans, capital ratios) or the latest bank failures (FDIC). earnings_calendar gives a company's results dates and the next one estimated, or who reports in a window. Ownership: insider_trades (Form 4), planned_insider_sales (Form 144, filed before an insider sells) and fund_holdings (a manager's 13F); large_holders (13D/13G: 5 percent holders and activists), investment_advisers (Form ADV); funds: fund_portfolio (what a mutual fund or ETF holds, its flows and returns, from N-PORT); private markets: private_offerings (Form D raises by startups and funds) and ipo_pipeline (S-1/F-1 registrations and priced IPOs); positioning: short_interest, off_exchange_volume (FINRA dark pools), fails_to_deliver (SEC) and futures_positioning (CFTC Commitments of Traders). Rates and the economy: economic_calendar (when the data comes out), fomc (the Fed's decisions, votes and projections), treasury_yields, treasury_auctions, federal_debt, economic_series (FRED), world_economy (World Bank, every country) and economic_outlook (OECD projections). Beyond the US: european_filings (a company's ESEF annual report, the figures it tagged), reference_rates (SOFR, EFFR, €STR, ECB and Bank of England rates from the central banks, and about forty central banks' policy rates), prediction_markets (Polymarket and Kalshi prices for an outcome) and crypto_fundamentals (DefiLlama: a protocol's value locked, fees and revenue, a chain's value locked, a stablecoin's supply) and funding_rates (perpetual funding and its annualized carry, OKX). Compute: compute_prices (what an hour of each data-centre GPU costs on demand across the clouds, and the Cirrun GPU Index). Options: options_chain, volatility_surface and futures_curve (Cboe delayed data). The market at a glance: market_overview (US indices and the VIX, the eleven sectors ranked by the month, Treasury yields, the dollar, oil, gold, bitcoin and markets abroad, each over a day, a week, a month, three months, the year to date and a year). Prices: price_history for stocks in the US and abroad, currencies, futures, indices and crypto (no key needed; the user's Alpaca or Tiingo key is used for US stocks when set). Every result has its source URL; cite it.";
